A mapped type iterates over an existing type's keys and builds a new type by transforming each one — the mechanism behind `Partial`, `Readonly`, `Record`, and others:

```typescript
type MyPartial<T> = { [K in keyof T]?: T[K] };
type MyReadonly<T> = { readonly [K in keyof T]: T[K] };

interface User { id: number; name: string; }
type PartialUser = MyPartial<User>;   // { id?: number; name?: string }
type FrozenUser = MyReadonly<User>;   // { readonly id: number; readonly name: string }
```

`keyof T` produces a union of `T`'s property names as a type (`"id" | "name"` for `User`); `[K in keyof T]` iterates that union, and `T[K]` (an **indexed access type**) looks up each property's original type. Adding `?` or `readonly` in the mapped type's declaration applies that modifier to every generated property uniformly — this is literally how the standard library's `Partial<T>` is defined, not an approximation of it.

Three mechanisms narrow a wide type down to a specific one, each suited to a different kind of union:

```typescript
function len(value: string | number[]) {
  if (typeof value === "string") return value.length;      // typeof — primitives
  return value.length;                                       // narrowed to number[] by elimination
}

function area(shape: Circle | Square) {
  if (shape instanceof Circle) return Math.PI * shape.radius ** 2; // instanceof — class instances
  return shape.side ** 2;
}

type Event = { type: "click"; x: number } | { type: "key"; code: string };
function handle(e: Event) {
  if (e.type === "click") console.log(e.x);      // discriminant field — plain objects
  else console.log(e.code);
}
```

`typeof` narrows primitives, `instanceof` narrows class instances, and checking a shared literal **discriminant field** (`type` here) narrows plain-object unions — the same discriminated-union pattern from the previous topic's `ApiResult<T>`. Picking the right one depends entirely on what shape the union members actually are.

```typescript
type Shape =
  | { kind: "circle"; radius: number }
  | { kind: "square"; side: number }
  | { kind: "rectangle"; width: number; height: number };

function area(shape: Shape): number {
  switch (shape.kind) {
    case "circle": return Math.PI * shape.radius ** 2;   // shape narrowed to the circle branch
    case "square": return shape.side ** 2;                 // narrowed to the square branch
    case "rectangle": return shape.width * shape.height;  // narrowed to the rectangle branch
  }
}
```

Each `case` narrows `shape` to exactly the union member whose `kind` literal matches, so `shape.radius` is only visible (and only valid) inside the `"circle"` case — accessing it in the `"square"` case would be a compile error.

